      {
        "sha": "238256aa05912d714c22f00515995aff8c707552",
        "node_id": "C_kwDOI5jSX9oAKDIzODI1NmFhMDU5MTJkNzE0YzIyZjAwNTE1OTk1YWZmOGM3MDc1NTI",
        "commit": {
          "author": {
            "name": "Natalie Jameson",
            "email": "natalie.jameson@discordapp.com",
            "date": "2023-04-14T17:09:28Z"
          },
          "committer": {
            "name": "GitHub",
            "email": "noreply@github.com",
            "date": "2023-04-14T17:09:28Z"
          },
          "message": "[transitions] Do not remove cpus from configured target graph (#2)\n\nUpstream converts ios_multi_cpus to a single value. rules_pods does\r\nnot. So when rules_pods objc_library rules depend on rules_ios rules,\r\nthey are considered different configurations, and we end up splitting\r\nthe configured target graph into two configurations. When swift, objc,\r\netc try to link in all objc_libraries later in the build, they then\r\ntry to link the same library twice, because the configured nodes are\r\nduplicated, and we run into duplicate symbol, module redefinition, etc\r\nerrors.\r\n\r\nIn theory, this could be reverted once the rules_ios migration is\r\ncomplete, but for our use case, it should not hurt anything being\r\npresent. This logic, also, only affects our opt builds that build\r\nfor multiple cpus. The development builds are unaffected.",
          "tree": {
            "sha": "1c8f9fe89f2e32a6e01aa171c3590e81d7dcd9e3",
            "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/discord/rules_ios/git/trees/1c8f9fe89f2e32a6e01aa171c3590e81d7dcd9e3"
          },
          "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/discord/rules_ios/git/commits/238256aa05912d714c22f00515995aff8c707552",
          "comment_count": 0,
          "verification": {
            "verified": true,
            "reason": "valid",
            "signature": "-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsBcBAABCAAQBQJkOYjICRBK7hj4Ov3rIwAABUsIAJvrLWwpK50NjGKBgmvKYOgM\nKry73dVVDezDJEzVsSV8NyLUYQ6TxV4t3i4YBR0j0MPsCkJ+WwgKOhsLtnzcGfxx\nr49PaWUMKU9bMGWOFp0M23Xd457BZ3vUg1wWrRc4dszfmkHajXmEJyU1a6zPCigd\njx7zwuqt0RqD851FK4qR+rXISQ+yqlM1klKLcoV8hLUUnIgy9dHJ2BfkLADBQDxF\nKyGtRUGe5OQiA7k0THbfeNZogHgGY/s8cd7WOgAn5Z8NeYdvzA1KRGrkbTKdJUk8\njj3LAJuIs4HyUazKVWlFh30oPs36chNVjrq4h83gl+j1He8bZIstuh9xWShIAq8=\n=Z8XM\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n",
            "payload": "tree 1c8f9fe89f2e32a6e01aa171c3590e81d7dcd9e3\nparent 429e3f0cf19b5ea51aada9f42211651615b13733\nauthor Natalie Jameson <natalie.jameson@discordapp.com> 1681492168 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1681492168 -0700\n\n[transitions] Do not remove cpus from configured target graph (#2)\n\nUpstream converts ios_multi_cpus to a single value. rules_pods does\r\nnot. So when rules_pods objc_library rules depend on rules_ios rules,\r\nthey are considered different configurations, and we end up splitting\r\nthe configured target graph into two configurations. When swift, objc,\r\netc try to link in all objc_libraries later in the build, they then\r\ntry to link the same library twice, because the configured nodes are\r\nduplicated, and we run into duplicate symbol, module redefinition, etc\r\nerrors.\r\n\r\nIn theory, this could be reverted once the rules_ios migration is\r\ncomplete, but for our use case, it should not hurt anything being\r\npresent. This logic, also, only affects our opt builds that build\r\nfor multiple cpus. The development builds are unaffected."
          }
        },
